<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Awjila</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Augila</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Awjila is an oasis town in the Libyan Desert of eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>, some 250 km inland from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-sirte">Gulf of Sirte</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> mentions it as Augila, where the Nasamones went each summer to harvest dates. It long served as a station on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caravan-trade">caravan routes</a> between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a>, and its 12th-century Atiq mosque is among the oldest in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/awjila.jpg" alt="Awjila" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: محمد الدرسي · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_farm_in_Awjilah.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Awjila is an oasis town in the Al Wahat District of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> in northeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/libya">Libya</a>. Since classical times it has been known for high-quality dates. With the adjoining oasis of Jalu it is isolated, the only towns on the desert highway between Ajdabiya, 250 kilometres to the northwest, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufra">Kufra</a>, 625 kilometres to the southeast.</p><p>An account of 1872 describes three oases, Awjila, Jalu to the east and Jikharra to the northeast, each with a small hill covered in date palms rising from a plain of red sand impregnated with salts of soda, and together holding 9,000 to 10,000 people. The people cultivate small gardens with water from deep <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/well">wells</a>. They are mainly <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berbers">Berber</a>, and some still speak a language of Berber origin, the Awjila language.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> describes the nomadic Nasamones, who moved between the coasts of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-sirte">Gulf of Sirte</a> and the oasis of Augila, where they may have exacted tribute. In summer they left their flocks by the coast and went to the oasis to gather dates. Other people lived there permanently. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strabo">Strabo</a> notes that it resembled the oasis of Ammon in its palms and water. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/procopius">Procopius</a>, writing around 562, reports that sacrifices to Ammon and to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> continued in two Libyan cities called Augila, and that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/justinian-i">Justinian I</a> turned the oasis's temples into churches.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabs">Arabs</a> took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrenaica">Cyrenaica</a> in 644, and the region around Awjila was conquered by Sidi Abdullah ibn Sa'ad ibn Abi as-Sarh, a companion of Muhammad, whose tomb was established there around 650. The chronicler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/al-bakri">Al-Bakri</a> records several mosques by the 11th century. By the 10th century Awjila lay on the route between the Ibadi Berber capital of Zuwayla in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>. In the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mamluk">Mamluk</a> era the route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> ran through Awjila to the Fezzan and on to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-kanem">Kanem</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bornu">Bornu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/timbuktu">Timbuktu</a>, and Awjila became the main market for slaves from those lands.</p><p>In 1639 it came under the Turkish ruler of Tripolitania. In the 18th century its merchants held a monopoly of the trade between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezzan">Fezzan</a>. From about 1809 a new route from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wadai-empire">Wadai</a> through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kufra">Kufra</a>, Awjila and Jalu to Benghazi bypassed the Fezzan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/darfur-sultanate">Darfur</a>. Muhammad ibn Ali as-Senussi stayed in Jalu and Awjila before opening his first lodge at al-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dmytro-vyshnevetsky">Baida</a> in 1843, and a Senussi centre was established at Awjila in 1872.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The Great Mosque of Atiq at Awjila is described as the oldest mosque in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sahara">Sahara</a>, in a style whose rooms stay cool in summer heat and warm at night. The oasis remains known for its dates, which come from many varieties of palm, and for its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tomato">tomatoes</a> and cereals. From the 1960s the oil industry drove the growth of the once sleepy village.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Awjila” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>They were herders who each summer travelled inland to the <b>Augila</b> oasis to harvest dates.</blockquote><a href="/state/nasamones">Nasamones</a></li></ul></section>
